.TH SLITSTEST 1
.SH NAME
slitstest \- check that danby/slits really produces interference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slitstest
.SH DESCRIPTION
The claim
.IR danby-slits (1)
makes is not that a pattern appears.  Any hole in any wall gives a
pattern.  The claim is INTERFERENCE: that what two slits give is not the
sum of what each gives alone, and in particular that the brightest point
on the screen is midway BETWEEN the slits, directly behind the wall,
where nothing came through.
.PP
So the same problem is run twice, once with both slits open and once
with one of them moved out of reach, and the two are compared.  It reads
the same spec file the program runs.
.SH "WHAT IT CHECKS"
.TP
.B With two slits, the maximum is at the midpoint
Measured at y = 9.60 against a midpoint of 9.60.
.TP
.B Max, min, max
A minimum either side of the central fringe and a secondary maximum
beyond that.  A single broad lobe would pass a test that only looked for
a central peak; this is what makes it a FRINGE.
.TP
.B The pattern is symmetric about the midpoint
Because the slits are.
.TP
.B With one slit, the maximum moves onto that slit
Measured at y = 11.40 against an open slit at 11.40.  This is the
control, and it is the one that matters: without it every check above
would pass on a model that produced a central blob for any reason
whatever \(em including no wall at all, which puts the packet's own
maximum at the midpoint.
.TP
.B The norm is conserved
0.998 and 0.996 across the two runs.  The solver has to still be
solving.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/slitstest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby-slits (1),
.IR tdsetest (1),
.IR pde (2)
.SH BUGS
It takes a few minutes: two full two-dimensional runs of two hundred and
forty steps each, and nothing about that is fast.
.PP
The fringe positions are not compared with a formula.  They could be \(em
.IR danby-twoslit (1)
has one \(em but the packet arrives over a spread of angles and
distances, so the comparison would need a bracket wide enough to be
worth little.  What is checked instead is the thing that distinguishes
interference from diffraction, which is where the maximum IS.
